  "findings": [
    "QN90A native root/app observed-completion medians were 185.371/165.634 ms versus prior 263.838/242.298 ms; contemporaneous managed medians remained lower at 140.982/76.565 ms.",
    "S90D native root/app observed-completion medians were 90.100/61.380 ms, but a native root sample had 1125.913 ms admission and 1324.662 ms observed completion. This new tail is un-attributed and retained.",
    "QN90A native warm launch remained slightly slower than managed. Full acquisition/public-path timing and stable tail distributions remain unqualified."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Runtime absence checks cover native helper runtimes and freezers, not historical service staging or sealed launch-fence tombstones retained by recovery.",
    "Two warmup pairs and four measured pairs per TV; nearest-rank p95 is the maximum and does not establish a stable tail distribution.",
    "Warm loopback measurements exclude root acquisition, HTTPS service scheduling and boot readiness.",
    "The previous S90D 1016.031 ms app-job admission outlier remains unexplained; these later samples do not erase it.",
    "File and directory sync before gate release and terminal persistence remain required; managed job writes provide different durability guarantees.",
    "Phase resource samples and kernel tails are not continuous peaks or full-run kernel capture. Exact idle wakeups, integrated acquisition/public-path performance and at least 24-hour mixed endurance remain open.",
    "Local fault tests model persistence interruptions; hardware pressure tests use the ordinary signed binary. No power-loss or exact write-boundary interruption claim.",
    "Restart failures without container init were reproduced with an unreaped worker zombie owned by PID 1; both ordinary and fault-enabled suites pass with --init. No process-absence guard was weakened.",
    "Managed selection, .NET entry/worker fallback and independent 300-second .NET rescue remain deployed. No reboot, standby, global policy change or push."
  ]
}
